  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 205 PM CDT Sun Aug 9 2026\n\nEarly this afternoon, a cumulus field remains focused north of the\narea along a theta\u2011e and instability gradient over  southeastern \nNebraska, with only a low probability that any activity brushes far \nnortheast Kansas as it moves east\u2011southeast with the mean flow. A \ncapped boundary layer and generally weak large\u2011scale ascent continue \nto limit convective initiation over most of the area, though a \nstrengthening low\u2011level jet and steep mid\u2011level lapse rates tonight \nmay allow isolated elevated thunderstorms if parcels can overcome \nlingering CIN. Given modest shear and instability, any storms that do\nform could briefly produce strong, possibly damaging winds, but the \noverall severe threat is constrained by the expected sparse coverage \nand capping.\n\nThe main story then shifts to heat as H85 temperatures warm toward 30\n\u00b0C beneath an expanding subtropical ridge aloft,  supporting \nafternoon highs in the upper 90s to around 100 across central and \nnortheast Kansas through at least Wednesday. Tomorrow\u2019s heat may be \nmildly mitigated by some high level clouds, but generally, heat \nindices around 100\u2013105 degree range continue to look possible through\nWednesday thanks to persistent moist southerly flow and an upper-\nlevel ridge nearby. \n\nBy midweek, guidance indicates the ridge begins to flatten as a\nfrontal zone drops into the central Plains, leading to gradually\nincreasing PoPs from Wednesday night onward. With increasing chances\nfor precipitation and a further south frontal zone, somewhat cooler\nhighs in the mid 80s to lower 90s are more likely.",
